'''''VisionQuest''''' is an American television [[miniseries]] created by [[Terry Matalas]] for the streaming service [[Disney+]], based on [[Marvel Comics]] featuring the character [[Vision (Marvel Comics)|Vision]]. A [[Spinoff (media)|spin-off]] from and sequel to the miniseries ''[[WandaVision]]'' (2021), it is [[List of Marvel Cinematic Universe television series (Marvel Studios)|the 18th television series]] in the [[Marvel Cinematic Universe]] (MCU) from [[Marvel Studios]] and is produced under its [[Marvel Television (Marvel Studios label)|Marvel Television]] label. The series shares continuity with [[List of Marvel Cinematic Universe films|the films of the MCU]]. It follows the [[synthezoid]] [[Vision (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Vision]] as he attempts to understand who he is by communicating with [[AI]] personas embedded in his programming, and goes on the run with a mysterious teenager who may be his reincarnated son. Matalas serves as [[showrunner]] and a director.

[[Paul Bettany]] reprises his role as Vision from previous MCU media, starring alongside [[James Spader]], [[Ruaridh Mollica]], [[Todd Stashwick]], [[Lauren Morais]], [[Orla Brady]], [[Diane Morgan]], [[Polly Frame]], [[Henry Lewis (playwright)|Henry Lewis]], [[Jonathan Sayer]], [[Emily Hampshire]], [[James D'Arcy]], [[Ben Crompton]], [[T'Nia Miller]], [[Art Malik]], [[Mary McDonnell]], and [[Joe Locke]]. Development of a Vision series began by October 2022, with ''WandaVision''{{'s}} [[Jac Schaeffer]] as [[head writer]]. She was no longer involved by May 2024, when Matalas was redeveloping the series and Bettany was confirmed to be starring. It was described as the final installment in a trilogy that began with ''WandaVision'' and continued with ''[[Agatha All Along]]'' (2024). Casting for the AI personas included some returning MCU characters and actors. Filming began by March 2025 and [[Wrap (filmmaking)|wrapped]] in July, taking place at [[Pinewood Studios]] in London with Christopher J. Byrne, Gandja Monteiro, [[Vincenzo Natali]], and Matalas directing episodes.

''VisionQuest'' premiered with its first two episodes on Disney+ on October 13, 2026, and ran for eight episodes, concluding on November 10. It is part of [[Marvel Cinematic Universe: Phase Six|Phase Six]] of the MCU.

== Premise ==
After being resurrected in ''[[WandaVision]]'' (2021), the [[synthezoid]] [[Vision (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Vision]] lives in hiding while he attempts to understand who he is by communicating with various [[AI]] personas that are embedded in his programming. When a bounty is placed on Vision's head by a mysterious person, he goes on the run with a mysterious teenager who may be his reincarnated son.<ref name="D232026Marvel" />

== Cast and characters ==
<!-- Cast order per main on end credits and WP:MCUTVCAST -->
* [[Paul Bettany]] as
** [[Vision (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Vision]]: A superpowered [[synthezoid]] and former [[Avengers (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Avenger]] created using the [[AI]]s J.A.R.V.I.S. and Ultron as well as the [[Mind Stone]]. Vision was killed in the film ''[[Avengers: Infinity War]]'' (2018) and then resurrected, with an all-white appearance and without his former memories or emotions, in the series ''[[WandaVision]]'' (2021).<ref name="MatalasRedevelop" /><ref name="BettanyMatalasOct2026DoG" /> After his memories were restored at the end of that series, Vision is now struggling to reconnect with those memories because he has no emotional attachment to them,<ref name="PremiereDate" /><ref name="BettanySep2026EW" /> with showrunner [[Terry Matalas]] calling him a "blank slate";<ref name="MatalasAug2026" /> he compared this journey to that of the ''[[Star Trek]]'' character [[Spock]] in the film ''[[Star Trek IV: The Voyage Home]]'' (1986).<ref name="MatalasSTLV2025Gizmodo" /> By the start of ''VisionQuest'', Vision has been posing as a human for years,<ref name="SetVisitCollider" /> and is now working at a pub under the name "Sherwood Ellis".<ref name="Eps1&2EasterEggsTF" /><ref name="Eps1&2RecapGizmodo" /> To cope with his [[identity crisis]], he retreats into his internal [[Mindspace (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Mindspace]], where various AI personas embedded in his programming manifest through human [[Avatar (computing)|avatar]]s.<ref name="Trailer2TIE" />
** The voice of Proto-One: An AI prototype created by Jo Carrington and her colleagues, which would serve as a template for J.A.R.V.I.S.<ref name="Eps5&6EasterEggsSR" />
** [[Phineas Horton (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Phineas Horton]]: A 20th century scientist and the creator of J.I.M.,<ref name="BettanySecretRoles" /> whom he built to replace his late son James. Phineas sold his [[family estate]] in England to fund J.I.M.'s creation, which led him to neglect his wife and daughter—ultimately causing them to leave him and move to America.<ref name="InvadersTease" /> By the 21st century, Phineas is regarded as the "great-grandfather of robotics" for his creation of J.I.M.<ref name="HumanTorchPhineasHortonExplained" />
** [[J.I.M.|J.I.M. / Synthetic Man]]: The world's first synthezoid,<ref name="BettanySecretRoles" /> built by Phineas in 1939 and modeled after his own image; Vision's human avatar is based on J.I.M.<ref name="InvadersTease" /><ref name="PeopleBehindAIs" /> He had a malfunction stemming from his "inability to process complex emotions" that caused him to undergo [[spontaneous combustion]], resulting in both Phineas' death and his own.<ref name="InvadersTease" /> Phineas' original [[source code]] for J.I.M. became the foundation for all the AIs that followed.<ref name="PeopleBehindAIs" />
* [[James Spader]] as
** [[Ultron (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Ultron]]: An AI created by [[Tony Stark (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Tony Stark]] and [[Bruce Banner (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Bruce Banner]] for a peacekeeping program, before developing a [[god complex]] and attempting to eradicate humanity. Since his android body was destroyed at the end of the film ''[[Avengers: Age of Ultron]]'' (2015),<ref name="Spader" /> Ultron has been uploaded by Vision into the Mindspace. There, he lives in a loft-like prison where he has access to the [[internet]] and spends his time painting,<ref name="SpaderAug2026SR" /><ref name="UltronAbsenceExplanation" /> which has given him a better perspective on humanity than Vision does;<ref name="MatalasUltronRetcon" /> Spader stated that Ultron is "probably the most human" of all the AIs in ''VisionQuest''.<ref name="BettanyOct2026Marvel" /> Matalas said the interactions between Vision and Ultron were a central dynamic for the series,<ref name="MatalasPHOct2025Dynamic" /> with Spader declaring that Ultron both loves and hates Vision,<ref name="UltronAbsenceExplanation" /> and that the two characters have a "sort of father-son-therapist-psychopath" relationship.<ref name="VisionKnowsWandaDead" /> Spader explored how Ultron had become a more humane AI since ''Age of Ultron'', retaining a conflicted relationship with humanity but developing a genuine understanding of it.<ref name="UltronEvolution" /> Spader said ''VisionQuest'' allowed for further exploration of Ultron compared to his role in ''Age of Ultron'', calling him one of the series' "driving forces" alongside Vision and Tommy Maximoff.<ref name="SpaderUltronDrivingForces" />
** Virgil Horton: Carrington's ex-husband and former business partner who is the grandson of Phineas. He was Paladin's client, placing a bounty on Vision's head.<ref name="ClientUnmasked" /><ref name="VirgilClient" /> He is also the human avatar for Ultron in the Mindspace.<ref name="SpaderNewCharacter" />
* [[Ruaridh Mollica]] as [[Tommy Maximoff (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Tommy Maximoff]] and [[Thomas Shepherd (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Thomas Shepherd]]:<br />Tommy Maximoff is the [[Speedster (fiction)|speedster]] son of Vision and Wanda Maximoff. In the series ''[[Agatha All Along]]'' (2024), his twin brother Billy Maximoff placed Tommy's soul into the body of Thomas Shepherd, a teenager who drowned.<ref name="NYCC2025EW" /><ref name="NYCC2025Variety" /><ref name="MollicaResume" /> In ''VisionQuest'', Tommy is an 18-year-old orphan who is newly out of the [[foster care system]] in London,<ref name="FinalProductionNotes" />{{rp|4}} and has been [[pickpocketing]] to survive, with Mollica stating that the character "likes to read and play people. There's a bit of [[Artful Dodger]]y."<ref name="SetVisitCollider" /> Tommy experiences visions stemming from his memories of Wanda's [[Hex (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Hex]] in ''WandaVision'', which leads him to face an identity crisis as he struggles to reconcile the personas of Tommy Maximoff and Thomas Shepherd.<ref name="MollicaColliderOct2026" /><ref name="MollicaVarietyOct2026" /> Mollica described Tommy as a grifter and small-time thief who is "lost in his life" and searching for a purpose,<ref name="MollicaSep2026BU" /> noting that the character has "had to build who he is by himself, and there's a loneliness to that, and a yearning for family".<ref name="MollicaVarietyOct2026" /> Mollica compared Tommy's journey of discovering his powers for the first time to that of [[Tobey Maguire]]'s [[Peter Parker (2002 film series character)|Peter Parker / Spider-Man]] in [[Sam Raimi]]'s film ''[[Spider-Man (2002 film)|Spider-Man]]'' (2002).<ref name="MollicaSpiderManComparison" /> Mollica drew inspiration from [[Matt Damon]]'s performance as Will Hunting in the film ''[[Good Will Hunting]]'' (1997) for Tommy's portrayal.<ref name="SetVisitCollider" />
* [[Todd Stashwick]] as [[Paul Denning (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Paul Denning / Paladin]]:<br />A bounty hunter with a mysterious past who is hunting Vision.<ref name="MatalasSTLV2025Gizmodo" /><ref name="FinalProductionNotes" />{{rp|6}} Trained in all forms of martial arts and weaponry, he is the latest in a "centuries-long line" of bounty hunters who have held the title of "Paladin",<ref name="PaladinExplained" /> which he was adopted into. Paladin's wife died after being struck by a bullet intended for him; furthermore, he has not seen his now-adult daughter for years, as he was one of the victims of [[the Blip]] and chose to keep his distance to ensure her safety.<ref name="StashwickOct2026Collider" /><ref name="RazaReturn" /> He wears an [[Adamantium (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|adamantium]] chest armor provided by the [[Ten Rings (organization)|Ten Rings Syndicate]].<ref name="Eps3&4EasterEggsTF" /> Matalas compared Paladin to the ''[[Star Wars]]'' bounty hunter [[Boba Fett]], while Stashwick described Paladin as a badass and said he was similar to previous roles that Matalas had written for him.<ref name="MatalasSTLV2025TM" /> Matalas drew inspiration from [[Tommy Lee Jones]]'s performance as Sam Gerard in the film ''[[The Fugitive (1993 film)|The Fugitive]]'' (1993) for Paladin's morally ambiguous personality.<ref name="SetVisitCollider" /> Eli Gray portrays a young Denning.{{cn|date=October 2026}}
* [[Lauren Morais]] as [[Lisa Molinari (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Lisa Molinari]]:<br />A pickpocket and Tommy's partner-in-crime, with whom she develops romantic chemistry.<ref name="SetVisitCollider" /><ref name="MoraisMorgan" /> Mollica described Lisa as the only family Tommy has ever known,<ref name="MollicaSep2026SR" /> as the two characters met on a [[prisoner transport vehicle]].<ref name="SetVisitCollider" />
* [[Orla Brady]] as [[F.R.I.D.A.Y. (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|F.R.I.D.A.Y.]]:<br />An AI created by Tony to replace J.A.R.V.I.S. as his personal assistant.<ref name="MatalasSTLV2025Gizmodo" /> She assists Vision with socialization issues, humor, and tactical ops,<ref name="New&ReturningCharacters" /> and is based on Alison Hogan, the secretary to Tony's father [[Howard Stark (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Howard Stark]], who helped raise Tony; Hogan is the human avatar for F.R.I.D.A.Y. in the Mindspace.<ref name="Eps3&4EasterEggsTF" /><ref name="PeopleBehindAIs" /> The series explores Tony's death in the film ''[[Avengers: Endgame]]'' (2019) from F.R.I.D.A.Y.'s perspective, with Matalas noting that the character was unable to protect the one person she had been programmed to protect.<ref name="MatalasIronManDeath" />
* [[Diane Morgan]] as Tactical A.I. / Handler:<br />Paladin's AI who operates from a simulation of a cramped, outdated "office" generated by a [[Fault (technology)|faulty]] server in [[Tajikistan]].<ref name="MorganOct2026RT" /> Stashwick described the Handler as the closest thing Paladin has to a friend, stating that the relationship between them is a "cracked-mirror version" of the one between Vision and E.D.I.T.H.<ref name="StashwickOct2026Collider" />
* [[Polly Frame]] as Fiona Guthrie: The owner of The Old Selkie, a traditional Scottish pub in [[Edinburgh]] where Vision works<ref name="Eps1&2RecapGizmodo" />
* [[Henry Lewis (playwright)|Henry Lewis]] as [[D.U.M.-E.]]:<br />A robot created by Tony to assist him in his workshop.<ref name="LewisSayer" /> In the Mindspace, D.U.M.-E.'s human avatar is presented as a [[handyman]], handling general maintenance and [[Fire suppression system|fire suppression]].<ref name="AICharacterGuide" />
* [[Jonathan Sayer]] as [[U. (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|U.]]:<br />A robot created by Tony to assist him in his workshop.<ref name="LewisSayer" /> In the Mindspace, U.'s human avatar is presented as a handyman, handling general maintenance and acting as a [[defragger]] and [[Video analysis|video analyzer]].<ref name="AICharacterGuide" /> Sayer stated that D.U.M.-E. along with U. function like [[twin]]s who are "super in sync", and added that both characters want to show the other AIs in the Mindspace that they are "equal and able to mix in with them".<ref name="SetVisitCollider" />
* [[Emily Hampshire]] as [[E.D.I.T.H.]]:<br />An AI created by Tony that ran an [[augmented reality]] security and defense system. After Tony's death, control of E.D.I.T.H. was briefly bequeathed to his protégé [[Peter Parker (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Peter Parker / Spider-Man]] in the film ''[[Spider-Man: Far From Home]]'' (2019).<ref name="Hampshire" /> Since then, E.D.I.T.H. has been "reprogrammed for fun",<ref name="FinalProductionNotes" />{{rp|5}} shifting to handle recreation, [[game theory]], and weaponry in the Mindspace.<ref name="New&ReturningCharacters" /> She is based on Edith Marks, a fighter pilot and Howard's personal [[crash test dummy]] whose husband, [[Edward Marks (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Edward Marks]], died in a plane crash; Edith serves as the human avatar for E.D.I.T.H. in the Mindspace.<ref name="Ep4ReviewDoG" /><ref name="EDITHOrigins" /> Hampshire described the character as "basically the embodiment of all the human thrill-seeking joy in life", as well as "part [[cheerleader]], part AI, part malfunctioning [[theme park]] ride".<ref name="FinalProductionNotes" />{{rp|5}}
* [[James D'Arcy]] as [[J.A.R.V.I.S.]]:<br />The first AI created by Tony, developed to serve as his personal assistant before being used to create Vision in ''Age of Ultron''.<ref name="NYCC2025" /> In the Mindspace, J.A.R.V.I.S.'s human avatar is a [[butler]] resembling [[Edwin Jarvis (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Edwin Jarvis]], who served as the inspiration for his creation,<ref name="D232026Empire" /><ref name="AICharacterGuide" /> and he acts as an older-brother figure to Vision;<ref name="Eps1&2EasterEggsSR" /> J.A.R.V.I.S. oversees security, [[housekeeping]], and [[encryption]] in the Mindspace.<ref name="New&ReturningCharacters" />
* [[Ben Crompton]] as Clancy: A guard on a prison bus that attempted to transport Tommy and Lisa to [[Saint Mabyn's Psychiatric Sanitarium (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Saint Mabyn's Psychiatric Sanitarium]]<ref name="Crompton" />
* [[T'Nia Miller]] as [[Jo Carrington|Josephine "Jo" Carrington / Jocasta]]:<br />A neuroscientist who co-led a five-person team responsible for developing the first stable synthetic consciousness at the company they founded together, [[Markridge Biotech]].<ref name="ButcherbirdKillerExplained" /> Following Carrington's death from a [[brain tumor]] in 2008, her husband Virgil joined [[S.W.O.R.D. (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|S.W.O.R.D.]] and secretly utilized the organization's resources—intended for the reconstruction of Vision's body, a process in which he also assisted, in ''WandaVision''—to create the synthezoid [[Jocasta (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Jocasta]], transferring Carrington's consciousness into her;<ref name="UltronVirgilHortonTT" /> in this form, she is cunning, powerful, and driven by revenge,<ref name="Miller" /> targeting her former colleagues under the guise of the serial killer known as the "Butcherbird Killer".<ref name="JocastaExplained" /> Miller described Jocasta as a badass.<ref name="MillerSep2026Diva" />
* [[Art Malik]] as Simon Moorhouse: A researcher of Markridge Biotech<ref name="Malik" />
* [[Mary McDonnell]] as Katerina Wells: The CEO of Markridge Biotech<ref name="ButcherbirdKillerExplained" /><ref name="BattlestarGalactica" />
* [[Joe Locke]] as [[Billy Maximoff (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Billy Maximoff]]: Vision and Wanda's sorcerer son and Tommy's younger twin brother whose soul inhabited [[William Kaplan (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|William Kaplan]]'s body after his death in ''Agatha All Along''<ref name="Locke" />

Additional actors in the series include [[Faran Tahir]] reprising his MCU role as [[Raza (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Raza al-Wazar]], the leader of the Ten Rings faction that kidnapped Stark in the film ''[[Iron Man (2008 film)|Iron Man]]'' (2008);<ref name="Tahir" /> Charlie Russell as Violette, Phineas' wife; and Dulcie Connor as Victoria, Phineas' daughter and Virgil's mother.<ref name="RussellConnor" /> Also appearing in the series are Joanne Randle as Rebecca Edwards;<ref name="Malik" /> Bettany's wife [[Jennifer Connelly]] as the voice of the AI [[D.E.E.D.E.E. (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|D.E.E.D.E.E.]],<ref name="Connelly2" /> and [[Brian Quinn (comedian)|Brian Quinn]] as Dale.<ref name="Quinn" /> Matalas makes a [[cameo appearance]] as an interviewer for the channel FXN News,<ref name="Eps3&4EasterEggsSR" /> and the character [[Watcher (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Watcher]] makes a non-speaking cameo in the series.<ref name="WatcherCameo" /> [[Robert Downey Jr.]] and [[Elizabeth Olsen]] make uncredited cameo appearances in [[Mid-credits and post-credits scenes in the Marvel Cinematic Universe|a mid-credits scene]] in the final episode as [[Victor von Doom (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Victor von Doom / Doctor Doom]] and [[Wanda Maximoff (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Wanda Maximoff / Scarlet Witch]], respectively.<ref name="VQPostCredits" /><ref name="MatalasNov2026GD" />

