== Production ==
=== Background ===
In March 2009, [[Marvel Studios]] hired writers to help come up with creative ways to launch its lesser-known properties, including the [[Marvel Comics]] character [[Vision (Marvel Comics)|Vision]].<ref name="Writers2009" /> [[Paul Bettany]] was cast as the [[Marvel Cinematic Universe]] (MCU) incarnation of [[Vision (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Vision]] for the film ''[[Avengers: Age of Ultron]]'' (2015). This version of the character is a [[synthezoid]] who is partially created from [[J.A.R.V.I.S.]], an [[AI]] assistant voiced by Bettany in earlier [[MCU films]]. Following Vision's death in the film ''[[Avengers: Infinity War]]'' (2018), Bettany was revealed to be portraying a reassembled and reactivated version of the character in the [[Disney+]] series ''[[WandaVision]]'' (2021). This version was referred to as "White Vision" or simply "The Vision",<ref name="BettanyWhiteVision" /><ref name="SchaefferETMar2021" /><ref name="TheVisionBettany" /> and has an all-white appearance, similar to when the comic book character was resurrected with an all-white body and no memories or emotions.<ref name="BettanyWhiteVision" />

[[Jac Schaeffer]], the [[head writer]] of ''WandaVision'', signed a three-year overall television deal with Marvel Studios in May 2021 to develop additional Disney+ projects.<ref name="SchaefferDeal" /> By October 2022, they were developing a series called ''Vision Quest'',<ref name="SneiderVisionQuest" /><ref name="DeadlineVisionQuest" /> which would center on Bettany's "White Vision".<ref name="DeadlineVisionQuest" /> Schaeffer began discussing ''Vision Quest'' during the development of another ''WandaVision'' [[Spin-off (media)|spin-off]] series, ''[[Agatha All Along]]'' (2024),<ref name="WinderbaumOct2026TP" /> and she was set as head writer for ''Vision Quest'' while working on that series.<ref name="DeadlineVisionQuest" /> Development of a Vision-centric series was rumored as early as March 2021<!-- The THR article says "two years ago" from Oct 2022, for the Giant Freakin Robot article, given the URL at that time listed the publish date as "2 years ago". However, the article discusses the content of WandaVision, and the earliest web archive of the article puts the publication date as March 23, 2021. That archive is here: https://web.archive.org/web/20210323160914/https://www.giantfreakinrobot.com/ent/exclusive-vision-series.html -->.<ref name="THRVisionQuest" /> The name "Vision Quest" was previously used for a comic book storyline in ''[[West Coast Avengers]]'' vol. 2 #42–45 (1988–1989);<ref name="SneiderVisionQuest" /><ref name="VarietyVisionQuest" /> it was unclear if the series would be a direct adaptation of that comic.<ref name="SneiderVisionQuest" /> A [[writers' room]] was set to be formed in early November 2022,<ref name="SneiderVisionQuest" /><ref name="DeadlineVisionQuest" /> to include Peter Cameron, [[Megan McDonnell]], and Eileen Shim.<ref name="CameronMcDonnell" /><ref name="Shim" /> Cameron and McDonnell worked on ''WandaVision'' and ''Agatha All Along''.<ref name="CameronMcDonnell" /> ''Vision Quest'' was expected to follow White Vision as he attempts to regain his memories and humanity, with potential for [[Elizabeth Olsen]] to reprise her MCU role as Vision's wife [[Wanda Maximoff (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Wanda Maximoff / Scarlet Witch]].<ref name="DeadlineVisionQuest" />

=== Development ===
[[File:Terry Matalas Photo Op GalaxyCon Austin 2023.jpg|thumb|upright|Showrunner [[Terry Matalas]] was hired by May 2024 to redevelop the series.]]
By October 2023, Marvel Studios shifted its creative philosophy to a more traditional television development process, moving away from head writers and beginning to hire dedicated [[showrunner]]s for its series.<ref name="MarvelStudiosTVOverhaul" /> By May 2024, Schaeffer was no longer developing ''Vision Quest'' due to her focus on making ''Agatha All Along'',<ref name="MatalasRedevelop" /> as well as for "personal reasons" according to Bettany,<ref name="BettanySep2026EW" /> with [[Terry Matalas]] joining to redevelop the series and serve as its showrunner. ''Vision Quest'' was the first live-action series to be [[Greenlight|greenlit]] by the studio in nearly two years, as well as the first to move forward under that new development approach.<ref name="MatalasRedevelop" /> Marvel Studios hired Matalas after being impressed with his work on the [[Star Trek: Picard season 3|third season]] of ''[[Star Trek: Picard]]'' (2023), with Marvel Studios president [[Kevin Feige]] being a particular fan of the ''[[Star Trek]]'' franchise.<ref name="MatalasRedevelop" /><ref name="FeigeMatalasFan" /> Matalas was also set to direct for ''Vision Quest'', alongside Christopher J. Byrne, [[Vincenzo Natali]], and ''Agatha All Along'' episodic director Gandja Monteiro.<ref name="Directors" />

Matalas met with Schaeffer upon joining the project, though he had indicated that she did not want to have any creative involvement in order for Matalas to have a "blank slate" to work with; Schaeffer was still credited as an executive producer on the series.<ref name="MatalasTHRNov2026" /> Marvel Studios executive [[Brad Winderbaum]] said in September 2024 that the series would be a "love letter" to the work Schaeffer had done, while being unique to Matalas.<ref name="WinderbaumEWSchafferMatalas" /> The series was still being referred to by Marvel as ''Vision Quest'',<ref name="Livanos" /><ref name="WinderbaumSep2024Name" /><ref name="Tahir" /> but ''[[The Hollywood Reporter]]'' said this was not its official title.<ref name="Spader" /><ref name="Stashwick" /> Disney was referring to the series as simply ''Vision'' by May 2025,<ref name="DisneyUpfronts2025BGR" /> before the title was officially announced as ''VisionQuest'' that October. The series has been described as the final installment of the trilogy that began with ''WandaVision'' and continued with ''Agatha All Along''.<ref name="NYCC2025" /> Describing the relationship to those other two series, Matalas said ''VisionQuest'' was more of a sequel to ''WandaVision'', while touching on some elements from ''Agatha All Along''.<ref name="MatalasSep2026Polygon" /> Marvel Studios's [[Mary Livanos]], who worked on those two series, returned as an executive producer for ''VisionQuest'',<ref name="Livanos" /> alongside Bettany, Schaeffer, and Byrne,<ref name="ProjectProfile" /> while Roopesh Parekh was a producer.<ref name="FilmingWrap" /> The series is released under Marvel Studios' "[[Marvel Television (Marvel Studios label)|Marvel Television]]" label.<ref name="MatalasRedevelop" />

=== Writing ===
A writers' room opened during the week of May 20, 2024,<ref name="MatalasTHR" /> featuring Cindy Appel, [[Michael Taylor (screenwriter)|Michael Taylor]], Christopher Monfette, Nicole Falsetti, and Matthew Okumura, alongside Matalas.<ref name="WGACredits" /> Matalas described ''VisionQuest'' as an adventure and mystery drama, while also having elements of [[Surreal humour|surreal comedy]]. The basis of the series was inspired by two scenes: the final conversation between Vision and his creator, the villainous AI [[Ultron (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Ultron]], in ''Age of Ultron''; and the "[[Ship of Theseus]]" discussion between the two versions of Vision in ''WandaVision''.<ref name="MatalasSep2026Polygon" /> Matalas stated that the former scene was the "launching point" for ''VisionQuest'', while Bettany described the series as a "mash-up of the ''[[The Adventures of Pinocchio|Pinocchio]]'' and ''[[Frankenstein]]'' stories".<ref name="BettanyMatalasOct2026DoG" /> Additional inspirations for ''VisionQuest'' included 1960s comic books featuring Vision,<ref name="USAHeraldSep2026" /> as well as the ''[[Vision (2015 comic book)|Vision]]'' (2015–2016) comic book series by [[Tom King (writer)|Tom King]].<ref name="VivVision" /> Matalas said that he was given a great deal of freedom regarding the story of ''VisionQuest'',<ref name="MatalasOct2026IGN" /> though Feige had requested that Vision be reunited with his son [[Tommy Maximoff (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Tommy Maximoff]] in the series.<ref name="FeigeMandate" />

''VisionQuest'' is set around 2028, 18 months after ''Agatha All Along'' and years after the events of ''WandaVision'',<ref name="Timeline" /><ref name="Timeline2" /> at the end of which Vision's memories were restored.<ref name="PremiereDate" /> It focuses on the character's struggle to reconnect with those memories because he has no emotional attachment to them,<ref name="NYCC2025EW" /><ref name="BettanySep2026EW" /> which leads him to go through an [[identity crisis]];<ref name="BettanyAug2026Empire" /><ref name="MatalasSep2026TVI" /> [[Emily Hampshire]] stated that the "whole show is really about identity".<ref name="BettanySpaderOct2026D23" /> As part of his attempts to understand who he is, Vision interacts with the human population disguised as one of them during the day, while at night he retreats into the [[quantum processor]]s of his own mind, where he has created his own [[Mindspace (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Mindspace]]—which manifests as a peculiar, gleaming white mansion, filled with environments that he can alter at will.<ref name="BettanySep2026EW" /><ref name="Mindspace" /> There, he communicates with various AI personas he rescued from the [[Avengers Compound (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Avengers Compound]] and which are embedded in his programming.<ref name="MatalasUltronRetcon" /> Vision urges all these AIs to assume human [[Avatar (computing)|avatar]]s, just as he does,<ref name="UltronHumanForm" /><ref name="USAHeraldSep2026" /> and they possess distinct characteristics that help him understand different human qualities and abilities.<ref name="BettanyAug2026Empire" /><ref name="BettanySep2026EW" /> Bettany said the AI personas are inside the Mindspace because Vision had "been saving and copying and pasting them to keep them alive", including Ultron, whom Bettany stated "has to be kept behind a pretty impressive [[Firewall (computing)|firewall]] because he's a psychopath";<ref name="BettanyColliderAIsInHead" /> this firewall keeps him locked away in the mansion's attic.<ref name="Mindspace" />

Ultron returns in ''VisionQuest'' following his apparent death in ''Age of Ultron'' because the creative team wanted to include a character who was "really strong for Vision to play off of", and since they did not have access to Wanda, Ultron was chosen instead.<ref name="VisionKnowsWandaDead" /> Matalas stated that the goal of the series was to have Vision and Ultron "dissecting humanity and what it means to be a father", noting that both characters "deal with connection": Vision has become disconnected from the love he once felt, while Ultron finds a connection of his own,<ref name="SpaderOct2026USAToday" /> in the synthezoid [[Jocasta (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Jocasta]],<ref name="Eps5&6RecapIGN" /> who, in the comics, was created by Ultron to be his bride, before she rejected him.<ref name="JocastaUltronChange" /> Bettany said that the story of ''VisionQuest'' revolves largely around the relationship between Vision and Ultron,<ref name="EverythingKnowTF" /> as the two characters share a complex dynamic that Bettany was eager to explore.<ref name="BettanySpaderOct2026D23" />

The series continues the ''WandaVision'' trilogy theme of exploring parenthood, specifically fatherhood. Winderbaum said ''VisionQuest'' was about three generations—a grandfather (Ultron), father (Vision), and son (Tommy)—and would explore if a man who had an abusive father could be a good father himself.<ref name="WinderbaumMarvelPodcastJan2026" /><ref name="BettanySep2026EW" /> Bettany said ''VisionQuest'' explores [[intergenerational trauma]] with fathers and sons, similar to how ''WandaVision'' explores grief, as well as "denial of pain, and denial of your own truth, and coming to terms with who and what you are".<ref name="BettanyThemes" /> Matalas stated that Ultron influences the way Vision interacts with Tommy by "encouraging [Vision] to be more human".<ref name="VivVision" /> Another synthezoid introduced in ''VisionQuest'' is [[J.I.M.|J.I.M. / Synthetic Man]],<ref name="BettanyHortonHumanTorch" /> whose body was used by [[Ultron]] to create Vision in the comics.<ref name="D232026BoothCommentary" /> Matalas noted that he was particularly interested in the complex father-son relationship between J.I.M. and his creator [[Phineas Horton (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Phineas Horton]]—a dynamic that echoes the relationships Vision has with both Ultron and Tommy. Matalas added that he spent more time writing and rewriting the conversation between Vision, J.I.M., and Phineas than any other scene in the series.<ref name="BettanyHortonHumanTorch" /> J.I.M. is not referred to by his original codename of "[[Human Torch (android)|Human Torch]]" from the comics in ''VisionQuest'' because, at the time the series was in development, the MCU film ''[[The Fantastic Four: First Steps]]'' (2025) had not yet been released, and Matalas did not want the audience to confuse the character with [[Johnny Storm (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Johnny Storm]], who also goes by the name Human Torch and appears in that film, portrayed by [[Joseph Quinn]].<ref name="BettanySecretRoles" />

Matalas said each episode of ''VisionQuest'' feels like a different kind of film, similar to how each episode of ''WandaVision'' pays homage to a different era of American [[sitcoms]].<ref name="MatalasPHOct2025DiffFilms" /> Winderbaum said Matalas "knows the lore" of the MCU and that ''VisionQuest'' would be rewarding for fans who have followed the franchise for years.<ref name="NYCC2025EW" /> Matalas described one scene in the [[series finale]] as "a Marvel dream".<ref name="MatalasPHOct2025Dynamic" /> The second [[Mid-credits and post-credits scenes in the Marvel Cinematic Universe|mid-credits scene]] in that episode shows [[Victor von Doom (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Victor von Doom / Doctor Doom]] "awakening" Wanda at [[Mount Wundagore (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Mount Wundagore]], seeking to claim her power for himself. Matalas stated that Wanda was the "missing component" for the finale, noting that the production team had discussed about a dozen different ways to bring her back, including having her appear in "[[dream]]like ways". He highlighted, however, that if Wanda had been included at an inappropriate moment, the episode would have ended up revolving around her; since the focus needed to remain squarely on Vision, the mid-credits sequence proved to be the "perfect way to address" Wanda. For Matalas, concluding ''VisionQuest'' with the "resurrection" of Wanda allowed the series' events to lead directly into the MCU [[Crossover (fiction)|crossover]] film ''[[Avengers: Doomsday]]'' (2026), while also enabling the very character who launched the franchise's Disney+ era with ''WandaVision'' to bring this particular phase to a close.<ref name="MatalasNov2026GD" />

An unused scene that Matalas wrote featured Vision and Ultron in a church, discussing the [[human soul]] and its meaning, with Matalas noting that "AI reflecting on humanity is your way into so many different kinds of stories" that the series did not get to explore.<ref name="SpaderOct2026USAToday" /> A piece of dialogue cut from ''VisionQuest'' would have established that Virgil Horton—Phineas' grandson, an original character introduced in the series—worked as a consultant on Project Ultron, which led to the creation of the eponymous character in ''Age of Ultron''; for this reason, Virgil serves as the human avatar for Ultron in ''VisionQuest''.<ref name="UltronVirgilHortonTT" /> Matalas said that [[Viv Vision]]—Vision's daughter in the comics—was not included in the series because he felt the character would not fit into the story of ''VisionQuest'', noting that her origin conflicted with the mental state Vision is in at the beginning of the series.<ref name="VivVision2" />

