Bettany was confirmed to be reprising his MCU role of Vision with the news of the series' redevelopment in May 2024.<ref name="MatalasRedevelop" /> In August, [[James Spader]] was set to reprise his role as Ultron from ''Age of Ultron''.<ref name="Spader" /> The character also appears as a human in ''VisionQuest'' because Matalas did not want him to look like a "[[Computer-generated imagery|CGI]] robot 24/7" incapable of expressing emotions or doing the things Spader can do with "just the twitch of an eyebrow".<ref name="UltronHumanForm" /> [[Todd Stashwick]]—who previously worked with Spader on the television series ''[[Boston Legal]]'' (2004–2008), as well as with Matalas on the series ''[[12 Monkeys (TV series)|12 Monkeys]]'' (2015–2018) and ''Picard''—joined the cast a month later.<ref name="Stashwick" /><ref name="StashwickOct2026SW" />

In January 2025, [[Faran Tahir]] was revealed to be reprising his role as [[Raza (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Raza al-Wazar]] from the first MCU film, ''[[Iron Man (2008 film)|Iron Man]]'' (2008).<ref name="Tahir" /> Following a five-month audition process,<ref name="MollicaScottishAccent" /> [[Ruaridh Mollica]] joined the cast in February as a character referred to as "Tucker",<ref name="Mollica" /> and [[T'Nia Miller]] was cast in the leading role of [[Jo Carrington|Josephine "Jo" Carrington / Jocasta]] in May.<ref name="Miller" /><ref name="ButcherbirdKillerExplained" /> Soon after, Hampshire was revealed to be portraying the key role of [[E.D.I.T.H.]],<ref name="Hampshire" /> an AI introduced in the MCU film ''[[Spider-Man: Far From Home]]'' (2019) where she was voiced by assistant editor Dawn Michelle King.<ref name="AIActorsCB" /> Matalas wrote the role with Hampshire in mind, as they had previously worked together on ''12 Monkeys''.<ref name="SetVisitCollider" /> Matalas revealed in August that Stashwick was playing the comic character [[Paul Denning (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Paul Denning / Paladin]], and that ''Picard'' star [[Orla Brady]] was portraying the AI [[F.R.I.D.A.Y. (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|F.R.I.D.A.Y.]], replacing [[Kerry Condon]] who voiced the character in several MCU projects.<ref name="MatalasSTLV2025Gizmodo" /> [[Mary McDonnell]] was revealed as part of the cast the next month,<ref name="McDonnell" /> playing Katerina Wells.<ref name="ButcherbirdKillerExplained" />

In October 2025, several more cast members were revealed: [[Henry Lewis (playwright)|Henry Lewis]] and [[Jonathan Sayer]] as [[D.U.M.-E.]] and [[U. (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|U.]], respectively, two robots seen in the MCU films;<ref name="LewisSayer" /> and [[James D'Arcy]] as J.A.R.V.I.S., the AI originally voiced by Bettany in the MCU films. D'Arcy portrayed [[Edwin Jarvis (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Edwin Jarvis]], the human inspiration for the AI J.A.R.V.I.S., in several MCU projects.<ref name="NYCC2025" /><ref name="AIActorsCB" /> Matalas hired Lewis and Sayer after seeing them perform in the play ''[[Peter Pan Goes Wrong]]''.<ref name="SetVisitCollider" /><ref name="FinalProductionNotes" />{{rp|6}} That same month, Mollica was revealed to be portraying Tommy Maximoff and [[Thomas Shepherd (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Thomas Shepherd]], taking over from [[Jett Klyne]] who portrayed Tommy in ''WandaVision'' and the MCU film ''[[Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of Madness]]'' (2022).<ref name="NYCC2025EW" /><ref name="NYCC2025" /> Mollica previously auditioned for the MCU role of [[Billy Maximoff (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Billy Maximoff]], Tommy's twin brother.<ref name="MollicaBillyRole" /> In November, [[Lauren Morais]] and [[Diane Morgan]] were respectively revealed to be portraying [[Lisa Molinari (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Lisa Molinari]] and the Handler, the latter an AI associated with Paladin.<ref name="MoraisMorgan" /><ref name="MorganOct2026RT" />

In November 2026, in addition to Vision, Bettany was revealed to also be playing both Phineas Horton and J.I.M. / Synthetic Man in the series,<ref name="BettanySecretRoles" /> after the latter made a non-speaking [[cameo appearance]] in the MCU film ''[[Captain America: The First Avenger]]'' (2011).<ref name="HumanTorchRetcon" /> Matalas had always planned for Bettany to play J.I.M. besides Vision—but not Phineas; various "[[stunt casting]]s" were considered before Bettany suggested taking on all three roles to "really understand the [[corrupted file]]s being passed down from OS systems and humans into robots, and then robots finally into humans in a visual way", and Marvel Studios approved the idea.<ref name="BettanySecretRoles" /> Similarly, Spader plays not only Ultron, but also Virgil Horton.<ref name="SpaderNewCharacter" /><ref name="VirgilClient" /> Additional castings include [[Polly Frame]] as Fiona Guthrie;<ref name="FinalProductionNotes" />{{rp|6}} Bettany's wife [[Jennifer Connelly]] as the voice of the AI [[D.E.E.D.E.E.]],<ref name="Connelly2" /> having previously voiced another AI character, [[Karen (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Karen]], in the MCU film ''[[Spider-Man: Homecoming]]'' (2017);<ref name="Connelly" /> [[Brian Quinn (comedian)|Brian Quinn]] as Dale, a character name that recurs for the actor whenever he appears in series for which Matalas serves as showrunner, such as ''12 Monkeys'', ''[[MacGyver (2016 TV series)|MacGyver]]'' (2016–2021), and ''Picard'';<ref name="Quinn" /> and [[Joe Locke]], [[Robert Downey Jr.]], and Olsen reprising their respective MCU roles as Billy Maximoff, Victor von Doom / Doctor Doom, and Wanda Maximoff / Scarlet Witch in the series finale.<ref name="Locke" /><ref name="VQPostCredits" /><ref name="MatalasNov2026GD" />

=== Design ===
Will Hughes-Jones served as the production designer for ''VisionQuest'',<ref name="Jones" /><ref name="SetVisitSR" /> with Sarah Young as the costume designer after she was an assistant costume designer on ''Age of Ultron''.<ref name="Young" /><ref name="SetVisitCollider" /> Hair, makeup, and [[prosthetic]]s designer Frances "Fran" Hounsom also worked on the series.<ref name="USAHeraldSep2026" />

Despite ''VisionQuest'' not using [[generative AI]], Hughes-Jones deliberately emulated the flawed aesthetic of AI software renderings in the design of the Mindspace, including stair banisters that lead nowhere, chairs built into walls, and wonky furniture.<ref name="NoGenAI" /> The exterior of the Mindspace appears as a crisp, white structure, echoing the color of Vision's body at the beginning of the series. For the mansion's exterior, Hughes-Jones drew inspiration from the architecture of [[Edinburgh]]—a place that has long held significance for Vision—as well as from the paintings of [[Edward Hopper]] and [[M. C. Escher]]. Hughes-Jones and his team initially envisioned the Mindspace as a house surrounded by other buildings on a busy urban street, though they ultimately decided that the mansion should stand on its own. The kitchen of the Mindspace features 40 custom-made products—each with its own unique branding—created specifically for ''VisionQuest'', including the Vis-O-Matic [[vacuum cleaner]], Infinidairy 2-percent milk,<ref name="Mindspace" /> Vision's Mustard, Firewall Hot Sauce, Mindstone Soup,<ref name="FinalProductionNotes" />{{rp|7}} and Vibranium Foil, the latter being a reference to the metal alloy [[Vibranium (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|vibranium]],<ref name="Eps1&2EasterEggsTF" /> the material from which Vision's body is constructed.<ref name="CastPeople" />

Ultron's attic in the Mindspace is filled with books, works of art, and a massive stack of 18 [[Cathode ray tube|CRT]] televisions, which he uses to monitor the outside world. In this space, there is also a six-armed statue of [[Julius Caesar]]—a nod to the difficulty AI-generated imagery often has in rendering arms, legs, and fingers. Hughes-Jones noted that the attic is encircled by 12 steel columns, which enclose the space like the bars of a prison; the tops of these columns were designed to evoke Ultron's robot jaw. Furthermore, Hughes-Jones remarked, "If you look above the windows in Ultron's loft, there are some circular windows, which are the same pattern as Vision's eyes in stained glass. So, he's always watching him." The production team debated for many weeks what the view outside the Mindspace should look like, until Hughes-Jones suggested it be a [[screensaver]] reminiscent of an iconic [[Windows Desktop]] image, featuring a landscape with a blue sky, rolling green fields, and fluffy clouds,<ref name="Mindspace" /> which Matalas described as having "vibrant, bright, almost ''[[Mario Bros.]]''-like colors".<ref name="FinalProductionNotes" />{{rp|7}} The view from the attic, however, differs from that of the rest of the mansion, with the skyline outside appearing as a blend of [[New York City|New York]], [[London]], and other cities. Spader stated that this detail "spoke to the [[dichotomy]] between Ultron and the rest of the inhabitants of the house".<ref name="Mindspace" />

Young approached ''VisionQuest'' with the idea that the costumes could visually represent AI. She had to recreate the White Vision costume for the series because the only two original examples remaining from ''WandaVision'' had deteriorated while in storage.<ref name="USAHeraldSep2026" /> Similarly, Vision's costume reflects the silhouette of his robotic form, including a jumper with the [[Mind Stone (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Mind Stone]] in its center.<ref name="SetVisitSR" /> Hounsom developed a custom paint mixture for Bettany as White Vision and recreated details associated with Vision's previous appearances in the MCU. For Ultron's human avatar, Young developed the character's costume in collaboration with Spader, incorporating robotic design elements into the coat's seams, buttons, and belt buckle. Spader also grew a beard, which was dyed to transition from darker shades to silver and white.<ref name="USAHeraldSep2026" /> Spader wanted three distinct looks for his different characters: an "unbelievable hairy" prisoner while playing Ultron's human avatar, a "strange mustached villain" for Virgil, and a "clean slate" for his final appearance at the end of the series.<ref name="MatalasNov2026GD" />

Tommy's costumes were designed based on the color palette of his counterpart from the comics [[Speed (character)|Speed]].<ref name="SetVisitSR" /> Tommy was originally planned to wear a superhero suit in the series finale, with Mollica noting that his measurements were taken specifically for the creation of the outfit. However, Matalas could not find a plausible way within the narrative for Tommy to appear in such a costume, and since he wanted to keep the series "as grounded in reality as possible", the character ended up wearing a green, [[tracksuit]]-like outfit, complemented by protective goggles.<ref name="TommySuperheroCostumeCut" /> E.D.I.T.H. possessed an extensive wardrobe consisting of 21 distinct outfits—more than any other character in the series—including those of a boxer, bullfighter, priest, race car driver, field medic,<ref name="USAHeraldSep2026" /><ref name="FinalProductionNotes" />{{rp|8}} and spy, among others;<ref name="Eps1&2EasterEggsSR" /> a consistent color palette of red, blue, and yellow was developed to maintain continuity despite the character's frequent changes in attire. Costume references for E.D.I.T.H. included [[Amelia Earhart]], female [[World War II]] pilots, [[Formula One]] branding, and [[Antique aircraft|vintage aviation]], all reinterpreted through the Mindspace aesthetic.<ref name="USAHeraldSep2026" /><ref name="FinalProductionNotes" />{{rp|8}} Paladin's clothing is deliberately less conspicuous than in the comics, allowing the character to blend into the crowd. His wardrobe included both tactical and street versions of his jacket.<ref name="USAHeraldSep2026" /> J.I.M.'s red suit was inspired by the comics design as well as the suit created for ''The First Avenger'', with that suit appearing in the background of the series, which Matalas described as "mostly a painted [[diving suit]]", so it was not featured as prominently. Clockwork designs and motifs were featured in the suit and original [[source code]] chip, which Matalas called "really fresh and fun and era appropriate" for what early attempts at artificial intelligence could be.<ref name="BettanySecretRoles" />

=== Filming ===
[[File:Hatfield House.jpg|thumb|right|alt=Exterior shot of Hatfield House in January 2002|Location filming took place around the United Kingdom, including at [[Hatfield House]].]]
[[Principal photography]] began by March 14, 2025, at [[Pinewood Studios]] in London,<ref name="FilmingStart" /> under the [[working title]] ''Tin Man'',<ref name="WorkingTitle" /> with Byrne, Monteiro, Natali, and Matalas directing.<ref name="Directors" /> [[Christopher Ross (cinematographer)|Christopher Ross]], Neville Kidd, and [[Ben Smithard]] were the cinematographers.<ref name="Ross" /><ref name="KiddSmithard" /><ref name="FinalProductionNotes" />{{rp|1}} [[Location shooting]] took place in and around London,<ref name="Jones" />{{Independent source inline|date=June 2026}} as well as at various sites across the United Kingdom.<ref name="FinalProductionNotes" />{{rp|6}} Practical sets for the series' interior sequences were constructed both on [[sound stage]]s and on the backlot at Pinewood Studios;<ref name="FilmingLocationsMW" /> notable examples included the Mindspace mansion and a set spanning two streets that represented the crime-ridden city of [[Madripoor (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Madripoor]], the latter of which featured the [[Princess Bar]].<ref name="SetVisitSR" /> The former set took 14 weeks to build and consisted of seven fully functional rooms (not including Ultron's attic)—kitchen, living room, dining room, entrance hall, hallway, [[mechanical room|boiler room]], and [[movie theater]]—all designed to be physically connected to one another, allowing for continuous camera movement.<ref name="Mindspace" />

From late March to early April 2025, filming took place at a local pub in the [[Chiltern Hills]], England, which served as the location for The Old Selkie pub. The set dressing included the [[Saltire]], the national flag of [[Scotland]], displayed outside the building.<ref name="FilmingLocationsMW" /> Other locations included [[Hatfield House]] in [[Hertfordshire]], England, which stood in for Horton Manor; and [[White Waltham Airfield]] in [[Berkshire]], England, used for scenes set in Paladin's jet hangar.<ref name="FinalProductionNotes" />{{rp|7}} The scene in which Vision finds Horton and J.I.M. in the Mindspace took three days to film because Bettany had to constantly change his makeup for the three characters.<ref name="BettanyHortonHumanTorch" /> Bettany's stunt double, John Macdonald, assisted him while filming the scene, reading the other two character's lines while Bettany was filming the other character.<ref name="BettanySecretRoles" /> Bettany ruptured 10 [[blood vessel]]s in one of his eyes while filming a scene in which he had to pretend he had a device attached to his head. The actor's eye remained in that state for two weeks, and the production team had to "take it out" of the final shots.<ref name="BettanyEyeBloodVessels" /> Filming [[Wrap (filmmaking)|wrapped]] on July 31, 2025,<ref name="FilmingWrap" /><ref name="FilmingLocationsMW" /> and was completed in August,<ref name="Jones" />{{third-party inline|date=November 2025}} when Matalas indicated that production spanned seven months.<ref name="MatalasSTLV2025TM" /> The series had a gross budget of $119.4&nbsp;million, with a net budget of $93.8&nbsp;million.<ref name="BudgetForbes" />

=== Post-production ===
Drew Nichols, Amelia Allwarden, Jamie Gross, Christopher Gay, and McKenna Cook served as editors for the series.<ref name="FinalProductionNotes" />{{rp|1}} John Haley served as visual effects supervisor, while Jonathan Barrass was the special effects supervisor. There were over 280 [[practical effect]]s in the series, including magnetic rigging for Vision's flight scenes and interactive [[LED]] reflections for the Mindspace walls.<ref name="FinalProductionNotes" />{{rp|8}} The Mindspace set was equipped with practical effects to represent the moments when Vision is injured in the outside world: lights flicker and emit sparks, and the walls shift, revealing [[Boiler (power generation)|boilers]] and machinery hidden within.<ref name="Mindspace" /> The production team also created a functional mechanical component—to be positioned beneath a prosthetic arm—which was capable of lighting up on camera.<ref name="USAHeraldSep2026" /> Despite having written and directed the series finale, Matalas did not film the episode's second mid-credits scene;<ref name="MatalasNov2026GD" /> this particular sequence was omitted from [[Screener (promotional)|press screeners]] made available prior to the episode's release.<ref name="VQPostCredits" />

=== Music ===
[[Mick Giacchino]], the son of frequent MCU composer [[Michael Giacchino]], was revealed to be composing the series' score in March 2026.<ref name="Giacchino" /> The score for the season was released digitally by [[Hollywood Records]] and [[Marvel Music (record label)|Marvel Music]] in two volumes: music from the first four episodes was released on October 22, 2026, while the second volume for the final four episodes was released on November 12.<ref name="SoundtrackVol1" /><ref name="SoundtrackVol2" /> The second volume also included the original ''UltraVision'' theme song, "Don't Kill the World" by [[Kristen Anderson-Lopez]] and [[Robert Lopez]] and performed by [[Jonathan Coulton]].<ref name="SoundtrackVol2" /><ref name="MatalasNov2026GD" /> Anderson-Lopez and Lopez previously wrote songs for ''WandaVision'' and ''Agatha All Along'', with Matalas asking them to create a theme song that was "an anthem that reminds people who have just had the {{em|worst}} day that it's okay to not kill the world".<ref name="MatalasNov2026GD" /> The series also incorporates the [[Avengers (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Avengers]] theme by [[Alan Silvestri]], the elder Giacchino's [[Spider-Man (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Spider-Man]] theme,<ref name="Ep6Credits" />{{rp|at=53:42}}{{third-party inline|date=November 2026}} as well as [[Christophe Beck]]'s score and the Lopezes' songs from ''WandaVision''.<ref name="SoundtrackEasterEggs" />{{better source needed|date=November 2026}} Prominent songs in the series include "[[The Last Farewell]]" by [[Roger Whittaker]], "[[Calypso (John Denver song)|Calypso]]" by [[John Denver]], and "[[Rhinestone Cowboy]]" by [[Glen Campbell]].<ref name="MatalasNov2026GD" />

