== Marketing ==
Footage from the series was included in a [[sizzle reel]] that was shown at Disney's [[Upfront (advertising)|upfront]] presentation in May 2025.<ref name="DisneyUpfronts2025BGR" /> Bettany and Winderbaum promoted the series during a [[Marvel Studios Animation|Marvel Animation]] and Marvel Television panel at [[New York Comic Con]] 2025, where footage was shown.<ref name="NYCC2025" /> Mekishana Pierre and Nick Romano at ''[[Entertainment Weekly]]'' noted the "trippy setting" of a mansion in Vision's mind where he sees personifications of other AIs from the MCU, comparing this to the "reality-warping vibes" of ''WandaVision'' and ''Agatha All Along''.<ref name="NYCC2025EW" /> The appearance of Spader as Ultron in both robot and human forms was also noted by commentators.<ref name="NYCC2025EW" /><ref name="NYCC2025Variety" /><ref name="NYCC2025IGN" /> Bettany and Matalas promoted the series during the [[53rd Saturn Awards]] ceremony in March 2026,<ref name="BettanyMatalasSCMarch2026" /> and Bettany appeared at Disney's May upfront presentation to announce the premiere date and show more footage.<ref name="PremiereDate" />

At Disney's [[D23 (Disney)#2026|D23]] convention in August 2026, a ''VisionQuest'' booth displayed a screen framed like a painting which showed a mysterious figure engulfed in flames. The image would [[glitch]] to show glimpses of Ultron. Commentators believed the fiery figure could be the original Human Torch.<ref name="D232026BoothCommentary" /> Also at the convention, Feige, Bettany, and Spader promoted the series during a panel and debuted the first trailer before it was released online.<ref name="D232026Marvel" /> The trailer, which featured the song "[[Losing My Mind]]",<ref name="LosingMyMind" /> was described as "trippy" by Kirsten Chuba and Aaron Couch at ''The Hollywood Reporter'',<ref name="D232026THR" /> "mind-bending" by James Hibbs for ''[[Radio Times]]'',<ref name="D232026RT" /> and "as weird, if not weirder, than ''WandaVision''" by Michael McWhertor of ''[[Polygon (website)|Polygon]]''.<ref name="D232026Polygon" />

A second trailer was released on October 1, 2026. Michael Walsh of ''[[Nerdist]]'' praised the trailer, calling it an "absolute banger" that presented ''VisionQuest'' as "surreal, mysterious, heartfelt, and funny", while also bringing "real stakes" for both the series' characters and the MCU as a whole.<ref name="Trailer2Nerdist" /> Bettany promoted the series at [[San Diego Comic-Con]] Málaga 2026 on October 3, where the first 22 minutes of the premiere episode were screened.<ref name="SDCCMalaga2026" /> Bettany further promoted ''VisionQuest'' alongside Spader, Mollica, Hampshire, and Matalas at New York Comic Con 2026 that same month, where the series' first episode was screened.<ref name="NYCC2026Marvel" /> Also part of that convention was an immersive [[pop-up exhibition]] titled "Controlled, Altered, Deleted: The ''VisionQuest'' Collection", which was on display at Studio 525 in New York on October 9 and 10. The exhibition featured parodies of famous paintings, such as [[Grant Wood]]'s ''[[American Gothic]]'' (1930) and Hopper's ''[[Nighthawks (painting)|Nighthawks]]'' (1942), among others.<ref name="ArtGallery" /> An episode of ''VisionQuest'' was screened at the [[Savannah College of Art and Design|SCAD]] [[Savannah Film Festival]]'s third annual TV Sidebar on October 26—an event at which Bettany received the Virtuoso Award.<ref name="SCADFilmFestival" />

== Release ==
''VisionQuest'' had its first two episodes screened at the 2026 [[BFI London Film Festival]] on October 12,<ref name="LondonFilmFestivalEmpire" /><ref name="LondonFilmFestivalSR" /> ahead of their premiere on Disney+ on October 13;<ref name="ReleaseSchedule" /> the series was originally scheduled to premiere on October 14.<ref name="PremiereDate" /> The remaining six episodes were released weekly in two-episode blocks, followed by the final two episodes releasing individually on November 3 and 10, respectively.<ref name="ReleaseSchedule" /> It is a part of [[Marvel Cinematic Universe: Phase Six|Phase Six]] of the MCU.<ref name="MoraisMorgan" />

== Reception ==
=== Critical response ===
On review aggregator [[Rotten Tomatoes]], 86% of 97 critics gave the season a positive review, and the average of rated reviews was 7.30 out of 10. The critics' consensus reads, "Picking up where ''WandaVision'' left off, this series investigates the humanity beneath the robot, where the artificial becomes reality with heart, deliberate emotion, and a clear-eyed ''VisionQuest''."<ref name="RottenTomatoes">{{Cite Rotten Tomatoes |id=visionquest |type=tv |title=VisionQuest |access-date=October 14, 2026}}{{cbignore}}</ref> [[Metacritic]], which uses a weighted average, assigned a score of 64 out of 100 based on 25 critics, indicating "generally favorable" reviews.<ref name="Metacritic">{{Cite Metacritic |id=visionquest |type=tv |title=VisionQuest |access-date=October 13, 2026}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

=== Viewership ===
[[Luminate (company)|Luminate]], which gathers viewership data from some smart televisions in the United States, informed that ''VisionQuest'' generated 639&nbsp;million minutes viewed during its first ten days of streaming.<ref name="LuminateViewership" /> [[Nielsen Media Research]], which measures streaming viewership on certain American television screens, reported that it ranked as the seventh most-streamed original series from October 19–25, with 490&nbsp;million minutes viewed.<ref name="NielsenMeasure" /><ref name="NielsenMeasureTHR" /> The following week, from October 26 to November 1, it ranked sixth, with 548&nbsp;million minutes of watch time.<ref name="NielsenMeasureTHR2" /> The finale of ''VisionQuest'' reached 3.7&nbsp;million views globally in a single day of streaming, marking a 44% increase over the penultimate episode and becoming the most-watched season finale for an MCU television series in three years—since the [[Loki season 2|second season]] of ''[[Loki (TV series)|Loki]]'' (2023).<ref name="FinaleRatingsVariety" />

== Podcast ==
''VisionQuest Official Podcast'', a companion podcast episode for the series, was released on November 10, 2026. Hosted by [[Iman Vellani]], who portrays [[Kamala Khan (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Kamala Khan / Ms. Marvel]] in the MCU, and Frank Alvarez, it featured a conversation with Bettany, Spader, Mollica, and Matalas. The episode was released on Disney+ and [[YouTube]], with an audio-only version available on [[List of podcast clients|various podcast platforms]].<ref name="CompanionPodcast" />

== Future ==
In September 2026, Matalas said he had ideas for how the story of ''VisionQuest'' could continue in future seasons,<ref name="MatalasMoreSeasons" /> while Bettany said the series was introducing a "new type" of Vision and would be a "springboard" for the character's future MCU appearances.<ref name="BettanySep2026EW" /> By November, there had been talks at Marvel Studios regarding the future of some of the characters from ''VisionQuest'' in the MCU.<ref name="MarvelTelevision5Lessons" />

